WEBVTT

00:00:05.885 --> 00:00:10.986
<v Amanda> Okay welcome or welcome back to
WordPress Accessibility Day everyone.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:14.800
My name is Amanda. I'm based
here in Cleveland, Ohio.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.899
It is not yet daylight out
so it seems a bit dark

00:00:19.447 --> 00:00:22.514
and I will be your host for
the next three sessions here.

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:27.381
I have the honor of
introducing the next session

00:00:27.405 --> 00:00:32.048
which is the Digital Transformation
or the Social Digital Transformation

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:33.929
with Merary Alvarado.

00:00:34.885 --> 00:00:38.096
A little bit about Merary.
She is a software engineer

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:42.331
and accessibility evangelist with
six plus years of proven experience

00:00:42.356 --> 00:00:46.322
in executing development and
leading accessibility processes

00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:49.900
in digital strategies
for Fortune 100 clients.

00:00:50.764 --> 00:00:54.858
She has collaborated to create
national standards and best practices

00:00:55.397 --> 00:00:57.547
to be applicable across Costa Rica

00:00:57.571 --> 00:01:00.340
for digital, educational
and physical environments

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.884
to improve their independent living.

00:01:03.509 --> 00:01:08.418
She is the co-lead of the Accessibility
Web Costa Rica a public group on Facebook

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:13.842
which develops digital content, training,
conferences, events, webinars in Spanish

00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:18.203
to create awareness about the importance
of accessibility in the digital era.

00:01:18.762 --> 00:01:22.466
So welcome to Merary, very
excited to hear her talk.

00:01:22.725 --> 00:01:26.739
Please feel free to add your questions
to the YouTube chat feed there

00:01:26.764 --> 00:01:29.241
and we can answer those at the end.

00:01:31.029 --> 00:01:32.161
So let's get started.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.449
<v Merary> Perfect! So thank you very
much for that introduction.

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:42.742
I would like to start
sharing my screen right now.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.058
Okay so, thank you so much
for that introduction.

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:02.957
I'm really happy to be here
participating right now in this event

00:02:02.982 --> 00:02:04.322
because it's the first one!

00:02:04.347 --> 00:02:06.934
I'm really happy to be participant

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.977
because it's important
at least for Costa Rica

00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:13.125
because we are from
a Latin American country

00:02:13.443 --> 00:02:18.186
being presenting today here
is really really great for us.

00:02:18.463 --> 00:02:21.713
So accessibility as a
Digital Transformation

00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:23.588
or Social Digital Transformation.

00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:25.303
What do we—

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.520
what we can say about that?

00:02:27.674 --> 00:02:30.940
So I would like to start telling a story.

00:02:31.648 --> 00:02:37.110
When I was 16 years old I was sat
down at the first row at a bus

00:02:37.135 --> 00:02:38.777
just waiting to go to the high school.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.592
And suddenly, one woman stood up in
front of the door bus carrying a baby.

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:49.689
She was started talking with her
hands and I didn't understand.

00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:53.494
I was shocked, neither the bus
driver he was shocked as well.

00:02:53.787 --> 00:03:00.327
And suddenly the answer of the bus
driver was close the door in her face.

00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:03.613
I just remembered the feeling.

00:03:04.292 --> 00:03:07.825
To feel so bad going to the high school

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:12.812
and I couldn't forget her face
behind the door with the baby.

00:03:12.837 --> 00:03:15.379
She wait for a couple of
seconds and then leave.

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:19.562
That experience for me was important

00:03:19.958 --> 00:03:25.568
and in terms of disabilities,
it was my first experience

00:03:25.592 --> 00:03:29.355
talking or interacting with a person
with disabilities.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.807
Even though I was 16 years old,

00:03:31.832 --> 00:03:36.777
I didn't have the opportunity to talk
before, with any person with disabilities,

00:03:36.802 --> 00:03:39.187
at that moment with a deaf person.

00:03:39.655 --> 00:03:45.481
One of the funny things is that
after like 10 years I met Martha.

00:03:45.897 --> 00:03:51.260
Martha was that woman that
was at that time, at that moment

00:03:51.285 --> 00:03:56.651
and she assigned me right now the sign that
we have in Costa Rica.

00:03:56.676 --> 00:03:59.988
She gave me my name for
the deaf community.

00:04:00.013 --> 00:04:03.468
So I would like to say thank you
very much for being here

00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:04.969
to watching my presentation.

00:04:04.993 --> 00:04:09.010
I'm super happy you are here
in YouTube just watching that.

00:04:09.035 --> 00:04:11.666
So I would like to start saying one thing.

00:04:12.389 --> 00:04:16.442
There is important to mention
a couple of important concepts

00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:19.826
that we need to understand when
we are talking about accessibility.

00:04:20.131 --> 00:04:24.143
Sometimes we assume that accessibility
is just technical aspects,

00:04:24.168 --> 00:04:28.539
but I think it implies more other concepts.

00:04:28.564 --> 00:04:31.545
For example, the first one that I
would like to start mentioning is

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:32.926
Inclusive Economy

00:04:32.969 --> 00:04:37.575
and you probably said, I'm sorry Merary
we are talking about accessibility.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.026
So what Inclusive Economy needs
to be related with that conversation?

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.190
Well a lot.

00:04:44.045 --> 00:04:48.967
When we are talking about inclusive
economies, we are talking about

00:04:48.992 --> 00:04:53.001
the balance to include all people,
doesn't matter the background,

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:56.672
doesn't matter the level
or savviness in technology

00:04:56.697 --> 00:05:02.137
or the way the color looks
like, the gender—doesn't matter.

00:05:02.489 --> 00:05:04.001
Neither the disability.

00:05:04.026 --> 00:05:07.792
So that's why talking about
inclusive economy is important,

00:05:07.817 --> 00:05:11.700
because we are entrepreneurs,
we have businesses,

00:05:11.724 --> 00:05:13.156
we lead some projects

00:05:13.182 --> 00:05:19.237
and thinking about this is really important
to create more accessible products

00:05:19.237 --> 00:05:22.569
and services to many users as possible

00:05:22.594 --> 00:05:28.190
and of course try to be more
inclusive with all of users.

00:05:28.842 --> 00:05:36.322
Now one of the other important concepts
that we need to understand is Disability.

00:05:36.855 --> 00:05:40.400
Because if we want to create
accessible content,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.534
we need to understand
what disability means.

00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:49.041
And actually an accessibility,
well sorry disability, is complex.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:50.819
It's not easy to understand.

00:05:50.844 --> 00:05:55.998
It has too many variables and
we definitely understand that.

00:05:56.349 --> 00:06:01.041
But one of the things that I really
need to mention here or to recall

00:06:01.066 --> 00:06:04.243
is that disability is not the problem.

00:06:04.591 --> 00:06:06.456
We need to understand
what disability means,

00:06:06.481 --> 00:06:08.472
but disability is not the problem,

00:06:08.497 --> 00:06:13.840
it's usually the infrastructure, the
way that we create the sites,

00:06:14.514 --> 00:06:15.931
the type of services.

00:06:15.955 --> 00:06:17.624
If they are so complex.

00:06:17.649 --> 00:06:20.867
So we need to create more
things that are accessible.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:27.333
So that's why it's important to mention
that the complexity of those combinations

00:06:27.358 --> 00:06:32.523
like the disability concept and
also what we have around of us,

00:06:32.548 --> 00:06:33.690
it's important.

00:06:34.008 --> 00:06:36.596
So I would like to make a question.

00:06:36.621 --> 00:06:38.948
What do you understand about disabilities?

00:06:39.333 --> 00:06:42.873
We already read about
the disability meaning,

00:06:42.897 --> 00:06:45.884
but what do you
understand about disability?

00:06:45.884 --> 00:06:49.140
Because if you don't understand the concept

00:06:49.503 --> 00:06:51.639
in the way that we expect it,

00:06:51.664 --> 00:06:53.978
we cannot create accessible websites.

00:06:54.003 --> 00:06:57.235
And you probably said, what—
what are you talking about?

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:01.844
Well, usually when we think
about disabilities, we think in a—

00:07:01.869 --> 00:07:04.496
we think about disabilities
with different perceptions.

00:07:04.521 --> 00:07:07.675
We have some bias or we have assumptions.

00:07:08.029 --> 00:07:11.923
We usually think about
disability in different levels.

00:07:11.923 --> 00:07:14.958
For example, in Costa Rica
sometimes I heard that said,

00:07:14.983 --> 00:07:16.720
oh poor babies, poor angels.

00:07:17.256 --> 00:07:21.512
And we are limiting people with
disabilities to access some information,

00:07:21.537 --> 00:07:26.359
because we are not properly
understanding what disability means.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:33.822
And for example, we usually associate that
disabilities are always people who is deaf

00:07:33.822 --> 00:07:37.837
or who is blind or who have
some physical disabilities,

00:07:37.862 --> 00:07:43.523
but we have a range of many disability
types that we need to understand as well.

00:07:44.651 --> 00:07:48.251
And other things that is
most related to business is

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:54.523
probably in our team or in our project,
we have a more accessible culture.

00:07:55.056 --> 00:07:56.598
But what about our clients?

00:07:57.111 --> 00:07:59.381
what about our business owners?

00:07:59.796 --> 00:08:01.802
what about our team members?

00:08:02.032 --> 00:08:03.611
what they think about disability?

00:08:03.636 --> 00:08:06.741
So they are understanding
what is disability

00:08:06.765 --> 00:08:08.622
and why it's important accessibility,

00:08:08.622 --> 00:08:11.319
when they are creating
wireframes, for example,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.783
or they are creating websites.

00:08:13.807 --> 00:08:16.182
So that's important to mention.

00:08:16.182 --> 00:08:19.599
So I would like to
just talk as a high level

00:08:19.624 --> 00:08:22.988
about the different
disabilities that we have.

00:08:23.013 --> 00:08:25.980
We have the permanent one
which is the one that we can see.

00:08:26.005 --> 00:08:29.487
We can see people who is blind
or people who is deaf

00:08:29.512 --> 00:08:31.508
or people with mobility impairments.

00:08:31.532 --> 00:08:33.071
That's the permanent one.

00:08:33.646 --> 00:08:36.805
Then we have the other which
is the one the temporary.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:41.804
For example, you are fan
of rugby, martial arts,

00:08:41.804 --> 00:08:46.000
or if you are fan of American football,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.257
so sorry, if you have an injury,
you will be disabled for a couple of days.

00:08:52.282 --> 00:08:57.395
You will not able to do the
things as the way you did before,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:01.341
so you need to understand that some of
the things will be difficult for you,

00:09:01.341 --> 00:09:03.195
but temporary.
Okay?

00:09:03.265 --> 00:09:05.703
Then we have the third one
and it's the situational

00:09:05.703 --> 00:09:09.184
and I would like to illustrate
that within the story.

00:09:09.184 --> 00:09:13.874
As you can notice, I have an a
strong accent, Costa Rica accent.

00:09:13.874 --> 00:09:17.645
The "r" is super strong
in Costa Rica accent.

00:09:18.178 --> 00:09:23.285
And I will like, I love to visit,
I would like to to visit Korea.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:28.080
And can you imagine me talking
in English with a Korean

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.852
citizen with also they
have a strong accent.

00:09:32.016 --> 00:09:35.815
Well yes, that would be a
situational disability,

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.677
but I would like to clarify that
is not physical, is not mental,

00:09:39.702 --> 00:09:42.505
it's just situational.
It's because the language.

00:09:43.103 --> 00:09:46.527
And finally we have the
invisible disabilities

00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:52.626
and nobody in this call is safe of that

00:09:52.651 --> 00:09:57.045
because everyone has been suffer
these kind of disabilities,

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.144
at any time in your life.

00:09:59.169 --> 00:10:01.835
So that's why it's
important to understand that

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.382
invisible disabilities
are really important now.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:07.749
And why is this important?

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:09.782
When we know what is disability,

00:10:09.807 --> 00:10:12.047
what are the types of disabilities,

00:10:12.072 --> 00:10:16.011
we can definitely understand what
are the Internet Disabilities.

00:10:16.571 --> 00:10:20.596
This classification
implies five main levels

00:10:20.621 --> 00:10:25.408
like Visual, Hearing, Mobility &amp;
Dexterity, Speech and Cognitive.

00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:29.664
Being Cognitive the most
common in internet.

00:10:30.251 --> 00:10:33.150
So also when we
understand disabilities,

00:10:33.174 --> 00:10:36.508
we need to understand
what will be the Governance Models.

00:10:36.912 --> 00:10:39.238
What will be the laws
that we need to cover.

00:10:39.238 --> 00:10:42.964
We talked a lot about Web
Content Accessibility Guidelines

00:10:42.989 --> 00:10:48.402
I know, so I didn't—I didn't
miss some of really great talks

00:10:48.427 --> 00:10:50.326
between yesterday and today,

00:10:50.351 --> 00:10:54.085
really great talks about
different normatives,

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:56.809
like Section 508 or WAI-ARIA,

00:10:56.833 --> 00:10:58.158
so that is great.

00:10:58.211 --> 00:11:01.196
So if we understand what
will be our governance model

00:11:01.843 --> 00:11:04.580
in terms of the digital
product that we are creating,

00:11:04.604 --> 00:11:06.391
that will be really great, because

00:11:06.416 --> 00:11:09.120
for example talking about
United States, for example,

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.397
we need to consider International Levels,

00:11:11.397 --> 00:11:15.947
Federal Levels or state levels
depending on the industry.

00:11:15.972 --> 00:11:21.370
Now taking—keeping in mind
the previous information,

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:27.920
what will be the Best Practices in terms
of accessibility to create better sites?

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.905
to be participant in inclusive economies,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:34.324
and also make this kind of
digital transformation?

00:11:34.834 --> 00:11:38.388
And I would like to start saying
you need to define the status quo.

00:11:38.894 --> 00:11:40.326
And why'd I say that?

00:11:41.096 --> 00:11:46.332
A couple of days back I needed
to start working with a project.

00:11:47.291 --> 00:11:52.671
I needed to just let some clients that
why it's important accessibility.

00:11:52.695 --> 00:11:54.329
And that's the question that I received.

00:11:54.354 --> 00:11:58.086
Do you know how much is the percentage
of people with disabilities

00:11:58.111 --> 00:11:59.288
who access the site?

00:11:59.888 --> 00:12:01.267
I feel that way.

00:12:01.526 --> 00:12:02.527
Why?

00:12:02.551 --> 00:12:07.894
Because some people think that
disability is just a number

00:12:07.919 --> 00:12:10.807
and this one—
this is not good.

00:12:11.176 --> 00:12:15.453
So we can just not disregard
people with disabilities,

00:12:15.453 --> 00:12:18.549
because yeah it's—is that it?

00:12:18.549 --> 00:12:22.524
No, we need to be really inclusive
when we are creating sites.

00:12:22.549 --> 00:12:27.606
So I would like to mention
this project The Valuable 500.

00:12:27.631 --> 00:12:31.435
This product project is
led by Caroline Casey

00:12:31.828 --> 00:12:37.077
which is great you need to listen to—
listening her talking.

00:12:37.102 --> 00:12:38.833
So she is great

00:12:38.857 --> 00:12:45.114
and she led on a specific
survey with different leaders—

00:12:45.139 --> 00:12:47.794
business leaders of great companies

00:12:48.213 --> 00:12:56.178
and one of the topics that
she discovered was that 66—

00:12:56.203 --> 00:13:01.551
sorry 56 percent of the global managers

00:13:01.576 --> 00:13:07.275
that are participating
on the business plans of their companies

00:13:07.299 --> 00:13:10.957
didn't think about disability.

00:13:10.982 --> 00:13:13.498
And as I mentioned before,
it's not accessibility,

00:13:13.523 --> 00:13:16.533
it's disability, the main
point on the agenda

00:13:16.558 --> 00:13:20.550
so you can start working
better accessible products.

00:13:20.909 --> 00:13:26.026
And I would like to invite you
to just go back one year

00:13:26.051 --> 00:13:30.808
and try to go work—to look
for the World Economic Forum,

00:13:31.037 --> 00:13:36.951
because that was for the first time
the entire World Economic Forum

00:13:36.976 --> 00:13:42.080
talking about inclusive
business, inclusion, diversity,

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:44.477
and they talk a lot about that.

00:13:44.503 --> 00:13:47.654
And they provide some
really great recommendations

00:13:47.678 --> 00:13:50.630
about what will be
good in terms of business

00:13:50.655 --> 00:13:52.700
and why accessibility is very important.

00:13:53.367 --> 00:13:59.354
So now going back to the Accessibility
Best Practices in terms of our sites,

00:13:59.731 --> 00:14:03.901
what we need to do first?
Well, try to Reshape the Bias.

00:14:04.343 --> 00:14:09.854
Try to include accessibility as part of
the culture of my team starting with me.

00:14:09.879 --> 00:14:12.745
If I did,
I am the leader, right?

00:14:12.745 --> 00:14:16.773
because it's important to
start thinking strategically

00:14:16.798 --> 00:14:19.626
and create best practices in our team.

00:14:19.651 --> 00:14:24.462
Now try to look for support
for executive managers.

00:14:24.487 --> 00:14:27.571
If they understand that
accessibility is important,

00:14:27.596 --> 00:14:31.289
you can all start creating
really great products.

00:14:31.314 --> 00:14:36.320
And of course you need to—
advices from experts in the area,

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.192
so you will learn more about what
will be the best strategy for you

00:14:40.217 --> 00:14:41.763
and for your digital product.

00:14:42.666 --> 00:14:48.268
Then we have the Scope. We can do a
lot of things with accessibility.

00:14:48.293 --> 00:14:51.135
We can include accessibility
in everything,

00:14:51.379 --> 00:14:56.518
but sometimes we are limited and we
have some misconceptions about that.

00:14:56.933 --> 00:14:59.765
We can create disruptive products,

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:03.721
innovative products just by
including accessibility.

00:15:03.984 --> 00:15:06.354
So accessibility will be great for that.

00:15:06.354 --> 00:15:11.396
Try to understand what is the business
need, what would be your audience,

00:15:11.421 --> 00:15:17.004
what type of project or service are
you providing to the clients?

00:15:17.004 --> 00:15:21.531
And try to include accessibility
in all the process.

00:15:22.121 --> 00:15:23.135
Okay?

00:15:23.177 --> 00:15:25.792
Now what what would be the cost?

00:15:25.817 --> 00:15:28.714
Well, sometimes I hear people say,

00:15:28.739 --> 00:15:32.985
accessibility is super expensive
and yes it could be expensive,

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:38.400
but if you include accessibility
until testing rounds,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:44.134
without including since the planning,
since their requirements validation,

00:15:44.159 --> 00:15:47.874
or since the— not including
in the design portion,

00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:51.699
it will be really difficult
to minimize the cost.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.697
So it will be really expensive
at that stages in the life cycle.

00:15:57.059 --> 00:16:00.962
But if you are including best
practices since the beginning,

00:16:00.987 --> 00:16:03.695
it will be really good for your teams.

00:16:04.548 --> 00:16:09.535
Now Test Test Test and Test
that's the most important.

00:16:09.843 --> 00:16:17.722
We cannot avoid the importance that
testing is the most critical part

00:16:17.747 --> 00:16:19.314
in terms of accessibility.

00:16:19.599 --> 00:16:26.659
Why? Because of the one hundred
percent, seventy percent of the effort

00:16:26.684 --> 00:16:31.023
and the notification or
the clarification for the users

00:16:31.048 --> 00:16:33.614
that the site is accessible or not,

00:16:33.639 --> 00:16:37.032
depends on manual testing strategies.

00:16:37.032 --> 00:16:40.981
So it's important to create
an specific strategy

00:16:41.006 --> 00:16:46.873
when we have trained resources that can
also provide really good recommendations

00:16:46.898 --> 00:16:48.660
in terms of accessibility,

00:16:48.685 --> 00:16:52.367
because it will be the specific
experience of the user,

00:16:52.367 --> 00:16:56.226
using assistive technology
and the digital product.

00:16:56.251 --> 00:17:01.374
So we will have a real perspective
about if our site is accessible or not.

00:17:01.773 --> 00:17:03.767
Then we have the automated tools

00:17:03.792 --> 00:17:07.197
which is another thing that we
will cover in the next slides,

00:17:07.502 --> 00:17:11.257
but also we need to try to
balance these two strategies

